UID:97901-1795863600-1795892400@bangaloreinternationalcentre.org
SUMMARY:Feather Fest 2026 | Day 1
DESCRIPTION:Have you ever seen or heard\nA poem as lovely as a bird?\n \nA celebration of the wonder and allure of birds\, this two-day festival is made for curious minds of all ages. Storytelling sessions\, workshops\, talks\, and competitions invite everyone to peek into the feathered world and let imagination take flight! \nThis is the second edition of “Feather Fest”\, presented by Early Bird as part of Young Birders’ Month\, a nationwide celebration of birds for young people. \nSchedule \n11am – 1pm | Birds\, Beaks and Big Smiles! – A Bird Cartooning Workshop (For Ages 7-13) | Library\nwith Dintoon\nA fun-filled workshop where kids learn to draw India’s most fascinating birds using simple shapes and expressive lines. From hornbills with giant beaks to owlets with enormous eyes\, every bird becomes a cartoon character with its own personality. No drawing experience needed\, just curiosity. Come ready to spread your creative wings! \n11:30am – 12:30pm | Bird Banter – Bioacoustics\, Filmmaking and a Shared Love of Birds (For Ages 14+) | Gallery\nwith Sugandhi Gadadhar\, Chiti Arvind & Suhel Quader\nA free-flowing conversation between a wildlife filmmaker\, an ecologist\, and a scientist and educator\, united by a deep love of birds and nature. Together\, they explore why birds draw them in\, as a subject of research\, a focus for filmmaking\, and a way to connect with the natural world. What is it about birds that keeps them listening\, watching\, and coming back? \n2pm – 3:30pm | Feathers and Colors – Early Bird Art Room (For All Ages) | Library\nEarly Bird Join-the-Dots worksheets turn bird identification into art! Connect the numbered dots\, colour in each outline\, and discover features like beak shapes\, crests\, tail lengths\, and markings\, making local species easy to spot and remember. This self-guided colouring activity comes with all supplies provided. Which bird will you reveal first?\n \n2pm – 3:30pm | Chirp Challenge – A Bird Imitation Competition (For Ages 10-15) | Gallery\nwith Krishna Murthy\, Samira Agnihotri\, Mittal Gala & Deepa Mohan\nCan you whistle like a magpie robin or call like a shikra? Budding birders\, talented mimics\, and bird lovers take the stage in a fun\, interactive competition celebrating attentive listening\, keen observation\, and the joy of copying the melodies of the wild. Who will sound most like the real thing?\n \n4pm – 5pm | A Little World of Origami Birds – Create Your Own Collection of Feathered Wonders (For Ages 9-12) | Library\nwith Roli Agrawal\nChildren learn to fold different origami birds\, uncover fun facts about their feathered friends\, and put together a personalised collection of paper birds to take home as a keepsake. A little creativity\, a little nature\, and lots of paper folding! \n4pm – 5pm | Listen up – From Hearing to Heeding (For Ages 14+) | Gallery\nwith Samira Agnihotri\nSound is a bridge between species\, shaping how humans and animals communicate\, perceive\, and share the world. This session explores how both experience their shared soundscapes. Can listening closely open pathways to coexistence?\n \nAcross the Weekend | 11am – 7pm \nBirds of Bangalore | Gallery (Only on Nov 28)\nThis art exhibition explores Bengaluru’s urban ecosystem through its birdlife\, asking how the design and growth of the city shape the lives of its many inhabitants\, human and otherwise. Birds are framed as fellow residents\, whose presence\, absence\, and adaptation say something about how we build and live in cities. A city’s ecological richness\, the exhibition suggests\, is as much a measure of its quality as its architecture and infrastructure. The exhibition is curated by Everyday Design Archive\, a decade-long initiative by JLX Studio that documents the living knowledge in everyday practices\, people\, materials\, and places. \nLet’s Go Birding through Philately | Board Room\nStep into a world where birdwatching meets philately in this unique exhibition curated by passionate stamp collector Ramu M. Srinivasa. Featuring a selection from his vast collection\, the exhibit showcases bird species across various global themes and taxonomy. The exhibition includes postal materials from the 18th century onwards. \nMeasure your Wingspan | Second Floor Foyer\nWingspan is the distance from fingertip to fingertip with your arms stretched out; and it’s the same for birds! To find yours\, stretch your arms in a T and compare to that of birds found around Bangalore.\n \nWhere Islands Take Flight – A Photo Exhibition | Second Floor Foyer\nAcross 836 islands and islets\, the Andaman and Nicobar archipelago is home to 28 endemic bird species found nowhere else in the world. This exhibition features photographs of endemics\, from the Nicobar megapode as it stirs at dawn to the Nicobar scops owl as its bright yellow eyes glow at night. Each bird is a small story of how life adapts to island homes\, and a reminder of why such fragile places deserve protection. This exhibit is curated by Roundglass Sustain\, an initiative of the Roundglass Foundation dedicated to raising awareness and supporting conservation in India’s natural world through evocative\, science-backed storytelling.\n \nWings of Wonder – An Exhibition on Raptors | First Floor Foyer\nEagles\, kites\, falcons\, and owls sit at the top of the food chain\, and their presence signals a healthy ecosystem. Protecting raptors helps safeguard many other species too. This exhibition by WWF-India opens a window onto the magnificent world of these hunters of the sky\, and shows why their conservation matters to every living thing beneath their wings.\n \nFlights of Imagination – An Art Exhibition | First Floor Foyer\nYoung bird lovers from across the country take flight with creativity in this vibrant showcase of the best entries from a national art competition. Hand-made artworks by children aged 11 to 16 celebrate the beauty and diversity of India’s migratory birds\, seen through youthful eyes. Each piece carries a little of the journey those birds make\, and a lot of the imagination of the artists who painted them. \nFeather Fest Photo Booth | First Floor Foyer\nA cheerful corner for festival memories\, whether posing solo or with family and friends. Bird-inspired outfits are always in the spirit of the day\, and a table of fun bird masks beside the booth adds a flutter of feathers to any look. Strike a pose\, show off your best avian style\, and let your colours shine! \nFind the schedule for Day 2 here! \nIn collaboration with: \n                        \n\n\nCelebrating Birds Through Stories\, Art and Beyond\nRSVP here (https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSe_ByWsGeRBOsAdGYoOwbquLGzpRCTafHRmcT_TRxhyrj4_Cw/viewform?usp=dialog)\n*Facilitators*: Dinesh Francis\, Sugandhi Gadadhar\, Chiti Arvind\, Suhel Quader\, Krishna Murthy\, Samira Agnihotri\, Mittal Gala\, Deepa Mohan\, Roli Agrawal\, Ramu M. Srinivasa\n*Event Categories*: Wildlife\, Workshops\, Young BIC
URL:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/event/feather-fest-2026-day-1/
LOCATION:Bangalore International Centre\, 7\, 4th Main Road\, Domlur II Stage\, Bangalore\, Karnataka\, 560071\, India
CATEGORIES:Wildlife,Workshops,Young BIC
